weight}\\n\"\n\nNow escape double quotes inside the string: we have single quotes, so double quotes only appear in dict keys? In Python code we used single quotes for strings; in the function we used double quotes? Actually we used single quotes for dict keys and string literals. So no double quotes inside, so no escaping needed. But there is a double quote in the raise ValueError? Actually we used single quotes for the message. So all strings are single-quoted, thus no conflict with JSON double quotes. But we have backslashes: we have \\n for newline. In JSON, backslash must be escaped. So we need to replace each backslash with double backslash. So \"\\n\" becomes \"\\\\n\". Also the backslash in the lambda? No lambda. Just the newlines. Also there is a double quote in the \"weight\" key? No, single quotes.\n\nBut careful: In the code string, we have \\n as literal newlines? Actually I typed the string with explicit \\n escapes for better readability. In the actual JSON representation, each newline must be escaped as \\\\n, meaning we need to write the JSON string with double backslash before n. But the JSON specification: a newline character is represented as \\n (single backslash) in a JSON string. So if we want the Python source to contain actual newline characters, we should encode them as \\n in the JSON string. But when we write the code string, we need to differentiate between the literal newlines in the Python source and the JSON escaping. The simplest approach: write the Python source as a single line with semicolons? Not good. Better to include actual newline characters in the code, and then when embedding in JSON, we escape them as \\n. So the JSON string will contain \\n each time there is a newline.\n\nSo I'll write the code with actual newlines for readability, then in the JSON payload, replace each newline with \"\\\\n\". But I need to produce the final answer as text, so I'll manually construct the JSON with escaped newlines.\n\nLet me produce the final JSON:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    core = case['core_distance']\\n    dist_list = case['distances']\\n    parent = {n: n for n in nodes}\\n    rank = {n: 0 for n in nodes}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x, y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry:\\n            return False\\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\\n            parent[rx] = ry\\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n        else:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n            rank[rx] += 1\\n        return True\\n    edges = []\\n    for u, v, d in dist_list:\\n        weight = max(core[u], core[v], d)\\n        if u > v:\\n            u, v = v, u\\n        edges.append((weight, u, v))\\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1], x[2]))\\n    mst_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n    for weight, u, v in edges:\\n        if union(u, v):\\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, weight])\\n            total_weight += weight\\n            if len(mst_edges) == len(nodes) - 1:\\n                break\\n    if len(mst_edges) != len(nodes) - 1:\\n        raise ValueError('Graph is disconnected; cannot compute MST')\\n    return {'mst_edges': mst_edges, 'total_weight': total_weight}\\n\"\n}\n\nBut note: In the code, we used single quotes in return dict, so no need to escape double quotes. However, the JSON string uses double quotes around the whole string, and we must escape any double quote inside the string. Since we have none, it's fine. Also backslashes: In the code we have \\n as escape sequences for newline? Actually we typed literal newlines, not backslash-n. So no backslashes in the source. So no need to escape backslashes.\n\nBut careful: The code string we wrote above includes literal newline characters. When we paste it into a JSON string, we must represent those newlines as \\n